1.1 Background Information
Poultry farming is one of the fastest-growing livestock enterprises in Kenya. Indigenous (Kienyeji) chicken meat is increasingly preferred by consumers because of its taste, nutritional value, lower fat content, and perceived health benefits.
In Thigio and surrounding areas, there is a growing demand for poultry products in homes, schools, hotels, butcheries, and local markets. However, poultry farmers face several constraints, including:
•	Increasing cost of commercial feeds.
•	Frequent disease outbreaks such as Newcastle disease and coccidiosis.
•	Water shortages during dry periods.
•	Cold stress during rainy seasons.
•	Fluctuating market prices.
•	Limited adoption of climate-smart livestock practices.
The Agriculture candidates at St. Mary's Girls Secondary School therefore designed a sustainable indigenous chicken broiler production enterprise to demonstrate climate-smart livestock farming practices and generate income through the sale of chicken meat and poultry manure.

